Pod Security Admission (the `PodSecurity` feature gate, KEP-2579) went through three stages: alpha, beta, then stable/GA.

- **Alpha (v1.22):** Released 2021-08-04. It was protected by the `PodSecurity` feature gate, which was disabled by default, and the built-in admission controller was also disabled by default. An initial set of E2E feature tests ran in an alpha test job.
- **Beta (v1.23–v1.24):** Released 2021-11-03, with the gate enabled by default. Beta criteria were:
  - resolving Windows restricted-policy support, versioning/deprecation of old profile versions, ephemeral containers support, and the PSP migration workflow;
  - re-assessing the API decisions on audit/warn modes;
  - filling test-coverage gaps;
  - including the admission plugin in the default enabled set. Enforcement stayed opt-in per namespace.
- **Stable/GA (v1.25):** GA was first targeted for v1.24, so users could migrate off PodSecurityPolicy before its removal in v1.25. The later KEP retargeted GA to v1.25. GA work included:
  - conformance considerations: PSA is not required for conformance;
  - user-experience improvements to warnings and messages;
  - adding the `pod-security.admission.config.k8s.io/v1` admission configuration API, unchanged from `v1beta1`.

The feature gate stayed listed as stable through v1.27 and was later removed.

Per KEP-3331, the flag-based OIDC configuration was "growing without bounds", and SIG Auth wanted to move to a proper versioned config format. A versioned config would make it easier to support features users had requested, such as:

- multiple ClientIDs;
- identifying users by more than one JWT claim;
- mapping any ID-token claim to user info extra attributes;
- failing authentication unless the user belongs to specific groups;
- array support in required claims;
- forwarding audiences to authorization.

The KEP's stated goals were to add:

- richer claim validation rules (the existing provider only validated the audience claim, and only by exact values);
- flexible claim mappings, including nested claims (flags could only pick a single value from a single claim and prefix groups);
- support for more than one OIDC provider and client ID without an external multiplexing provider;
- changing authenticator settings without restarting kube-apiserver;
- validation rules on the final user info object, so infra providers could safely expose this to customers;
- easy migration from the existing flags.

The flags were not removed outright. The new approach is backwards-compatible and the `--oidc-*` flags still work, but they are mutually exclusive with `--authentication-config`. Setting both makes the API server report an error and exit. SIG Auth recommends migrating to the file for its added flexibility and extensibility.
